 {:db/id "q-111398" :question/id #uuid "7095989b-ba77-5498-ade8-c79cbe233a38" :question/math-academy-id "q-111398" :question/problem "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nThe given matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. So, the equation $Ax=0$ corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd) and ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable corresponding to the non-pivot column. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}\\in R.$ This implies that\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate the $\\text{Null}(A).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-111398/ma-source-field/6de7f3c7a7dfbe796cd2f81c82e716658eb67fb4c61acaf0f82893a676cd0f4e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
