 {:db/id "q-112815" :question/id #uuid "c78b7fa0-e6a0-5d03-8538-f39afab9a911" :question/math-academy-id "q-112815" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=7{x}^{3}+42{x}^{2}$ have multiplicity $2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $7{x}^{3}$ and $42{x}^{2}$ is $7{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =7{x}^{3}+42{x}^{2} \\\\  & =7{x}^{2}(x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $x$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x+6)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-6$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-112815/ma-source-field/81fc82caa0bf6394cf90436ce8090c4c35912a406f200247c30b3c645bc82957"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
