 {:db/id "q-112968/ma-source-field/a5651193701fa3c0e3c0fe0264815cb5b650afd6e4d3d219e3fa01aeac274220" :answer-field/id #uuid "a41a2631-5f05-5cb0-8884-5d26ad87d7dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-112968/ma-source-field/a5651193701fa3c0e3c0fe0264815cb5b650afd6e4d3d219e3fa01aeac274220/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" "q-112968/ma-source-field/a5651193701fa3c0e3c0fe0264815cb5b650afd6e4d3d219e3fa01aeac274220/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd" "q-112968/ma-source-field/a5651193701fa3c0e3c0fe0264815cb5b650afd6e4d3d219e3fa01aeac274220/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" "q-112968/ma-source-field/a5651193701fa3c0e3c0fe0264815cb5b650afd6e4d3d219e3fa01aeac274220/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" "q-112968/ma-source-field/a5651193701fa3c0e3c0fe0264815cb5b650afd6e4d3d219e3fa01aeac274220/695792c47319cb1ed098a6d83038b7ad6d032838f9103d693bf346f1dc234ff8"] :answer-field/correct "q-112968/ma-source-field/a5651193701fa3c0e3c0fe0264815cb5b650afd6e4d3d219e3fa01aeac274220/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-112968" :question/id #uuid "584a8f77-ca02-5f77-bbb3-b9ad8836332c" :question/math-academy-id "q-112968" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{6}-7{x}^{2}+x-6,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to the lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{4},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-112968/ma-source-field/a5651193701fa3c0e3c0fe0264815cb5b650afd6e4d3d219e3fa01aeac274220"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
