 {:db/id "q-113517" :question/id #uuid "d34fadec-e1db-5f0e-b3fb-64f3326f1376" :question/math-academy-id "q-113517" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{(9-5x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (9-5x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=9-5x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (9-5x)^{-3}\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}){u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10{u}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{10(9-5x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(9-5x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot (-2)(9-5x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9-5x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(9-5x)^{-3}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =(9-5x)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(9-5x)}^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-113517/ma-source-field/5ea5575ab7144816deb3ac46a291c44351cc9f1abd8ce993c9750bb0d159c297"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
