 {:db/id "q-115706" :question/id #uuid "69d6e7a9-706b-5fd1-9cdb-c1c105740396" :question/math-academy-id "q-115706" :question/problem "![](images/88/883a60e9ac0d526d76243a44cff129f04ca148c1c2f0fb6a4b34db630b2193cd.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(x-a)(x-b).$ Find the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/01/012fe788fa1ac778b361fe62c3f70344546c6c5008fa6dcce536cc721d4ba5e9.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x+2)(x-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-2,b=1$ and $a+b=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-115706/ma-source-field/6fa9c40795a81e1614ff035456d27cf9e743013c2b5a16ccf208fe8d02c1482f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
