 {:db/id "q-116051/ma-source-field/fb702f1ba880a8130a77c4c5016ffc0399e8cc9d10b882fbb8763b0b8a3a0ddc" :answer-field/id #uuid "015670c7-de29-5d58-b9b4-055602c56ffa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-116051/ma-source-field/fb702f1ba880a8130a77c4c5016ffc0399e8cc9d10b882fbb8763b0b8a3a0ddc/2988eb935b0c3e8aec7e62ee98879dcc40dce13116f3cbe31166f18c84c6eeab" "q-116051/ma-source-field/fb702f1ba880a8130a77c4c5016ffc0399e8cc9d10b882fbb8763b0b8a3a0ddc/2be24b258d1c8100b297597cacf7db0cb6f22f9e2491846693e273412246b9b3" "q-116051/ma-source-field/fb702f1ba880a8130a77c4c5016ffc0399e8cc9d10b882fbb8763b0b8a3a0ddc/24b48021cf80db563b5722d7a3e7367d77395b8361afec40083d73db9f266a9d" "q-116051/ma-source-field/fb702f1ba880a8130a77c4c5016ffc0399e8cc9d10b882fbb8763b0b8a3a0ddc/57830b767ec09b48f89a0e524ced4fb0bec4a22601d54eb5965992f2a223ad9a" "q-116051/ma-source-field/fb702f1ba880a8130a77c4c5016ffc0399e8cc9d10b882fbb8763b0b8a3a0ddc/298f26c4929b070d732f8e6897c3be93aae04c4ac5bf26c825e1a606edb42849"] :answer-field/correct "q-116051/ma-source-field/fb702f1ba880a8130a77c4c5016ffc0399e8cc9d10b882fbb8763b0b8a3a0ddc/2be24b258d1c8100b297597cacf7db0cb6f22f9e2491846693e273412246b9b3" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-116051" :question/id #uuid "3c40d4d8-93cd-5fd9-9c9b-8b9eaffb8fe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-116051" :question/problem "![](images/30/30b101fa9812c49237ab72c0c4aebd1666df6de80367eb2ee59b33fe0e422d87.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square decrease at a rate of $3\\sqrt{2}\\text{ m}\\text{/min}$, at what rate is the perimeter decreasing?" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $3\\sqrt{2}\\text{ m}\\text{/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-12\\text{ m/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $12\\text{ m}/\\text{min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-116051/ma-source-field/fb702f1ba880a8130a77c4c5016ffc0399e8cc9d10b882fbb8763b0b8a3a0ddc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
