 {:db/id "q-116499" :question/id #uuid "d6db5db2-50cf-5e89-b77b-4e40b77e862d" :question/math-academy-id "q-116499" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a03b31de4f99e5e3ad4a65645beeeb067afc9fdfb0ccb39ada3aff305947f9d.png)\n\nThe length of the rectangular prism shown above is increasing at a rate of $7\\text{ in/min}.$ What is the rate of change of the surface area of the rectangular prism?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, and let $S$ be its surface area. The variables $x$ and $S$ change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =8\\text{ in}, \\\\ z & =12\\text{ in}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =7\\text{ in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nS=2(12)(x)+2x(8)+2(12)(8)=40x+192.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =40(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the above expression using $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=7,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =40(7)=280{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rectangular prism's surface area is increasing at a rate of $280\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-116499/ma-source-field/c062300b3df7617d909b89f070cf03c93fab233be41551223f11c8a7d1c78ec4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
