 {:db/id "q-117969/ma-source-field/405e567d94261bb0306af645a87b3114c064889d2841d59407e0359a7232881e" :answer-field/id #uuid "63a67a32-debb-51c3-9081-1309e01431cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-117969/ma-source-field/405e567d94261bb0306af645a87b3114c064889d2841d59407e0359a7232881e/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-117969/ma-source-field/405e567d94261bb0306af645a87b3114c064889d2841d59407e0359a7232881e/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-117969/ma-source-field/405e567d94261bb0306af645a87b3114c064889d2841d59407e0359a7232881e/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-117969/ma-source-field/405e567d94261bb0306af645a87b3114c064889d2841d59407e0359a7232881e/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-117969/ma-source-field/405e567d94261bb0306af645a87b3114c064889d2841d59407e0359a7232881e/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :answer-field/correct "q-117969/ma-source-field/405e567d94261bb0306af645a87b3114c064889d2841d59407e0359a7232881e/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-117969" :question/id #uuid "0f77be12-c79a-5d32-a315-5527f2d659be" :question/math-academy-id "q-117969" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(y-1),\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ and $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(y-1)=1\\cdot (1-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(y-1)=2\\cdot (1-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(y-1)=3\\cdot (1-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table. \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-117969/ma-source-field/405e567d94261bb0306af645a87b3114c064889d2841d59407e0359a7232881e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
