 {:db/id "q-120844" :question/id #uuid "0588ded7-d4bd-5616-b35b-33100943aa76" :question/math-academy-id "q-120844" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$  determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int -\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(4)=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{4}{(4)}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =1+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{4}{t}-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{t}-\\frac{2}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{t} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ t & =\\frac{12}{2} \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=6\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120844/ma-source-field/6748fe4b65fa5ab2646b8a810410f3ce54f8b222ae383e29482866450d5f9fd2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
