 {:db/id "q-122663/ma-source-field/5eaa799e5e411deb6a82b12e3a79e7e2e3fe97e0ad678fe661272c4ab40f3aa5" :answer-field/id #uuid "48ae7e1a-6a16-5754-8819-ca18faede750" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-122663/ma-source-field/5eaa799e5e411deb6a82b12e3a79e7e2e3fe97e0ad678fe661272c4ab40f3aa5/0825dcb7a69ebabd5bb74e00d5edddbbd0b41b66432679e41fe9631f6ee66ad6" "q-122663/ma-source-field/5eaa799e5e411deb6a82b12e3a79e7e2e3fe97e0ad678fe661272c4ab40f3aa5/401c3c4316729c507b622c5a9b2a55417262449150fc2ff6a25ec2a7eb04d608" "q-122663/ma-source-field/5eaa799e5e411deb6a82b12e3a79e7e2e3fe97e0ad678fe661272c4ab40f3aa5/fba306cd078c2281b6de4fb6b4c1064ce0681091bc4e2dd63e5f83b8422493a5" "q-122663/ma-source-field/5eaa799e5e411deb6a82b12e3a79e7e2e3fe97e0ad678fe661272c4ab40f3aa5/455fc28822738f730b71dc47f30bb2d6ffc6c168b01f2a7dfd73a9b23efc0eb8" "q-122663/ma-source-field/5eaa799e5e411deb6a82b12e3a79e7e2e3fe97e0ad678fe661272c4ab40f3aa5/e26c548e81959f6718d306c77b83baf2dad0c9ba4a863f0d37f854f5c1d9e807"] :answer-field/correct "q-122663/ma-source-field/5eaa799e5e411deb6a82b12e3a79e7e2e3fe97e0ad678fe661272c4ab40f3aa5/e26c548e81959f6718d306c77b83baf2dad0c9ba4a863f0d37f854f5c1d9e807" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-122663" :question/id #uuid "f9d1b0c7-3a1c-573d-9729-ab5857b0ca01" :question/math-academy-id "q-122663" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{1/x}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-{y}^{2}),\\,Q(x,y)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{1/x}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,x>{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator of the exponent vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}\\,.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-122663/ma-source-field/5eaa799e5e411deb6a82b12e3a79e7e2e3fe97e0ad678fe661272c4ab40f3aa5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
