 {:db/id "q-123292" :question/id #uuid "811bbc59-9fc3-5b4b-bd9e-2e09752150a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-123292" :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨{e}^{2t}+5,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩$, then $F(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨{e}^{2t}+5,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{2t}+5,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int ({e}^{2t}+5)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+5t+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+5t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+5t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+1)⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-123292/ma-source-field/c270f1b3a42d76855142494dee0eca2f418589f51ec3a49c540b40b176814b66"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
