 {:db/id "q-123294" :question/id #uuid "cbfe4f27-7d39-5346-b6c3-d28ca1cafdae" :question/math-academy-id "q-123294" :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨4{t}^{3}+2,\\,\\frac{t}{4}⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨4{t}^{3}+2,\\,\\frac{t}{4}⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨4{t}^{3}+2,\\,\\frac{t}{4}⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int (4{t}^{3}+2)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\frac{t}{4}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{4}+2t+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{4}+2t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{4}+2t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-123294/ma-source-field/b8b0d70eb2acd759c5514d0292e01ba6adca19be80ce0fbc610df4735e5b4386"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
