 {:db/id "q-123320" :question/id #uuid "83aa510b-e988-5498-be95-547a695eb498" :question/math-academy-id "q-123320" :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨{csc}^{2}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨{csc}^{2}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{csc}^{2}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int {csc}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+{c}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-123320/ma-source-field/5fca22b4efb64eb34ef9781124c5a187655eb4ff55857d205d03881e7d4392d7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
