 {:db/id "q-126166" :question/id #uuid "153b6d05-924f-5553-b86b-f60ba5f8d6c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126166" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{{2}^{2n}(n!)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2n+2}}{{2}^{2n+2}((n+1)!)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{2n}(n!)^{2}}{{x}^{2n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}(n+1)^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ Since $0<1$ for all $x,$ we conclude that $R=\\infty .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126166/ma-source-field/c4c707edbadf5fdf6f04ee5260ab272e61a5207f7e2e45c2d56df1683fb9bd53"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
