 {:db/id "q-127598/ma-source-field/36ffad73cd4b037904f85cabe4f213d5380d031b2bc72de119e59d881fe7b39c" :answer-field/id #uuid "d103e9f6-a851-56bd-8b50-38d9ebf224a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-127598/ma-source-field/36ffad73cd4b037904f85cabe4f213d5380d031b2bc72de119e59d881fe7b39c/34753b22b76591c1ca7269c8ce358838ff4b9da2ca798e04380229d8c49794e0" "q-127598/ma-source-field/36ffad73cd4b037904f85cabe4f213d5380d031b2bc72de119e59d881fe7b39c/b2f0965dae5c3b3856d9338e44e5e5404d1285ed1bf70896b860082cefe46f54" "q-127598/ma-source-field/36ffad73cd4b037904f85cabe4f213d5380d031b2bc72de119e59d881fe7b39c/bd4c08f0cab0cc77a4a4009435431f58da5787c715a0137c985e6469e36d6873" "q-127598/ma-source-field/36ffad73cd4b037904f85cabe4f213d5380d031b2bc72de119e59d881fe7b39c/d5e55947c3e746c10730d4def9a1743c912e7db6c0bff67d1c14fca37d389c3a" "q-127598/ma-source-field/36ffad73cd4b037904f85cabe4f213d5380d031b2bc72de119e59d881fe7b39c/fc736c88f73a4907600b6a849093e7520900e0ca6911d2556e96fe961f18d43a"] :answer-field/correct "q-127598/ma-source-field/36ffad73cd4b037904f85cabe4f213d5380d031b2bc72de119e59d881fe7b39c/bd4c08f0cab0cc77a4a4009435431f58da5787c715a0137c985e6469e36d6873" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-127598" :question/id #uuid "811d16c6-f719-59c2-bae0-963973f1dd90" :question/math-academy-id "q-127598" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=⟨1,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{({e}^{t}+1)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}+1}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{{e}^{0}}{({e}^{0}+1)^{2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{{e}^{0}+1}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}q(0)+\\frac{1}{2}{q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩+\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩+2⟨1,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,0,-1⟩}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{2},1⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(1(1)+\\sqrt{2}(0)+1(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(1+0-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127598/ma-source-field/36ffad73cd4b037904f85cabe4f213d5380d031b2bc72de119e59d881fe7b39c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
