 {:db/id "q-127600/ma-source-field/b9c1f50c89d7454061a14d36e4fb0a534c87f35529cd6a49efd90d9420ad0714" :answer-field/id #uuid "07a28c00-4228-59a1-bd55-b03951c2d0fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-127600/ma-source-field/b9c1f50c89d7454061a14d36e4fb0a534c87f35529cd6a49efd90d9420ad0714/23d6eb10ea16b2fe9c2e857ef5c0e51f1310635779296a0b62b08af7515d1069" "q-127600/ma-source-field/b9c1f50c89d7454061a14d36e4fb0a534c87f35529cd6a49efd90d9420ad0714/cc13829a4e396f8ad37124356207300cad8a0d3cd307ae851e14a95ca535ff7b" "q-127600/ma-source-field/b9c1f50c89d7454061a14d36e4fb0a534c87f35529cd6a49efd90d9420ad0714/52c1612c9a22627d023af4081b2292c327043326879aed302432edb380052e0f" "q-127600/ma-source-field/b9c1f50c89d7454061a14d36e4fb0a534c87f35529cd6a49efd90d9420ad0714/86cf2a9cef2c8df0e50ad3c2c643aa72ffdc8a0678b6b6ca0feee2ea9e4ee42e" "q-127600/ma-source-field/b9c1f50c89d7454061a14d36e4fb0a534c87f35529cd6a49efd90d9420ad0714/f7530a2abefc24722c990cca004da5d8b292e4e3760bd097c1525ffa018dc049"] :answer-field/correct "q-127600/ma-source-field/b9c1f50c89d7454061a14d36e4fb0a534c87f35529cd6a49efd90d9420ad0714/86cf2a9cef2c8df0e50ad3c2c643aa72ffdc8a0678b6b6ca0feee2ea9e4ee42e" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-127600" :question/id #uuid "0d57e43b-681d-5aa1-a53c-acdd85847d41" :question/math-academy-id "q-127600" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=0$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0)=\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k,\\,{q}^{′}(0)=-2\\,j+2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{2(0)}{({0}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(0)+\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =0(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k)+\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\,j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(0)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-j+k}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\cdot N(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}q(0)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2}\\,i+j+k)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-j\\cdot j+k\\cdot k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}(-1+1) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-127600/ma-source-field/b9c1f50c89d7454061a14d36e4fb0a534c87f35529cd6a49efd90d9420ad0714"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
